# Delete All Occurrences in a Doubly Linked List

### Problem

Given a doubly linked list and a target value, delete all nodes whose value is equal to the target.

### Approach

* First handle all target nodes present at the **head**.
* For the remaining list, traverse using `temp`.
* Check `temp->next` because the current node is used as the previous node for deletion.
* When the next node contains the target:

  * Connect `temp` with `D->next`.
  * Update the `prev` pointer of the next node.
  * Disconnect and delete `D`.
* If no deletion happens, move `temp` forward.
* Keeping `temp` at the same node after deletion helps handle **consecutive target nodes**.

### Time Complexity

**O(n)** — Every node is visited at most a constant number of times.

### Space Complexity

**O(1)** — No extra data structure is used.

### Code

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* deleteAllOccurrences(ListNode* head, int target) {
        ListNode* temp = head;

        while(head != NULL && head->val == target) {
            ListNode* D = head;
            head = head->next;

            if(head != NULL) {
                head->prev = NULL;
            }

            delete D;
        }

        temp = head;

        while(temp != NULL) {
            if(temp->next != NULL && temp->next->val == target) {
                ListNode* D = temp->next;
                temp->next = D->next;

                ListNode* N = D->next;

                if(N != NULL) {
                    N->prev = temp;
                }

                D->next = NULL;
                D->prev = NULL;
                delete D;
            }
            else {
                temp = temp->next;
            }
        }

        return head;
    }
};
```

### Mistakes & Learning

* Compared a `ListNode*` with an `int`:
  `temp->next == target` ❌ → `temp->next->val == target` ✅
* Forgot that the **head itself can be the target**.
* Forgot to handle `D->next == NULL` for the last node.
* Used `delete d` instead of `delete D` — C++ is case-sensitive.
* After deleting a node, moving `temp` immediately can skip **consecutive target nodes**.
* Initially used a singly linked list style of thinking. In a DLL, `prev` is directly available, so deletion can also be done by reconnecting both neighboring nodes.

### Limitation

This approach works correctly, but the deletion logic is slightly more complicated because it handles **head nodes separately** and then checks `temp->next`.

Since this is a **doubly linked list**, a cleaner approach is to traverse the current node and directly delete it using its `prev` and `next` pointers. This can reduce the special-case handling.
